{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[E]Lots of folks back East, they say, is [A]leavin' home every day,
[B]Beatin' the hot old dusty way to the California [E]line.
[E]'Cross the desert sands they roll, [A]gettin' out of that old dust bowl,
They [B]think they're goin' to a sugar bowl, but here's what they [E]find
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
Now, the [B]police at the port of entry say,
[B]"You're number fourteen thousand for this day."
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
Oh, if you [E]ain't got the do re mi, folks, you ain't got the do re [B]mi,
Why, you better go back to beautiful Texas, Oklahoma, Kansas, Georgia, [E]Tennessee.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[E]California is a garden of Eden, a paradise to live in or [A]see;
But [E]believe it or not, you won't find it so hot
[E]If you ain't got the [B]do re [E]mi.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
[E]You want to buy you a home or a farm, [A]that can't deal nobody harm,
Or [B]take your vacation by the mountains or [E]sea.
Don't swap your old cow for a car, you [A]better stay right where you are,
[B]Better take this little tip from [E]me.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 6}
'Cause I [B]look through the want ads every day
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 7}
But the headlines on the papers always say:
Oh, if you [E]ain't got the do re mi, folks, you ain't got the do re [B]mi,
Why, you better go back to beautiful Texas, Oklahoma, Kansas, Georgia, [E]Tennessee.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 8}
[E]California is a garden of Eden, a paradise to live in or [A]see;
But [E]believe it or not, you won't find it so hot
[E]If you ain't got the [B]do re [E]mi.
{end_of_verse}
